1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,760
Collin: I haven't seen you for a bit, Marie.

2
00:00:02,340 --> 00:00:05,040
Marie: No. I've been busy with my project.

3
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:09,060
Collin: You're making a vegan alternative to eggs, aren't you

4
00:00:09,180 --> 00:00:11,960
Something that doesn't use animal products?

5
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:17,100
Marie: Yes. I'm using chickpeas. I had two main aims

6
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:19,860
when I first started looking for an alternative to eggs,

7
00:00:20,140 --> 00:00:24,020
but actually I've found chickpeas have got more advantages.

8
00:00:24,420 --> 00:00:24,960
Collin: Right.

9
00:00:25,780 --> 00:00:30,100
Marie: But how about your project on reusing waste food

10
00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:32,720
you were looking at bread, weren't you?

11
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:37,300
Collin: Yes. It's been hard work, but I've enjoyed it.

12
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:39,640
The basic process was quite straightforward

13
00:00:40,280 --> 00:00:44,920
breaking the stale bread down to a paste then reforming it.

14
00:00:45,380 --> 00:00:48,480
Marie: But you were using 3-D printing, weren't you

15
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:50,200
to make the paste into biscuits?

16
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:54,140
Collin: Yeah, I'd used that before, but in this project,

17
00:00:54,260 --> 00:00:58,000
I had time to play around with different patterns for the biscuits

18
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:00,760
and finding how I could add fruit

19
00:01:00,760 --> 00:01:04,140
and vegetables to make them a more appetising colour

20
00:01:04,520 --> 00:01:07,860
and I was really pleased with what I managed to produce.

21
00:01:08,460 --> 00:01:16,720
Marie: It must've been a great feeling to make something appetising out of bits of old bread that would've been thrown away otherwise.

22
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:23,040
Collin: It was. And I'm hoping that some of the restaurants in town will be interested in the biscuits.

23
00:01:23,780 --> 00:01:25,380
I'm going to send them some samples.

24
00:01:26,280 --> 00:01:30,760
Marie: I came across something on the internet yesterday that might interest you.

25
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:36,160
It was a company that's developed touch-sensitive sensors for food labels.

26
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:37,410
Collin: Mmm?

27
00:01:37,860 --> 00:01:40,960
Marie: It's a special sort of label on the food package.

28
00:01:41,661 --> 00:01:43,432
When the label's smooth,

29
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:48,140
the food is fresh and then when you can feel bumps on the label

30
00:01:48,380 --> 00:01:50,340
that means the food's gone bad.

31
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:55,280
Marie: It started off as a project to help visually impaired people know

32
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:57,340
whether food was fit to eat or not.

33
00:01:58,260 --> 00:02:01,020
Collin: Interesting. So just solid food?

34
00:02:01,340 --> 00:02:04,880
Marie: No, things like milk and juice as well.

35
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:11,660
But actually, I thought it might be really good for drug storage in hospitals and pharmacies.

36
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:14,880
Collin: Right. And coming back to food

37
00:02:15,140 --> 00:02:19,860
maybe it'd be possible to use it for other things besides freshness.

38
00:02:19,980 --> 00:02:23,860
Like how many kilograms a joint of meat is, for example.

39
00:02:24,060 --> 00:02:27,060
Marie: Yes, there's all sorts of possibilities.

40
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:35,680
Collin: I was reading an article about food trends predicting how eating habits might change in the next few years.

41
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:40,060
Marie: Oh - things like more focus on local products?

42
00:02:40,580 --> 00:02:45,600
That seems so obvious, but the shops are still full of imported foods.

43
00:02:45,860 --> 00:02:48,880
Collin: Yes, they need to be more proactive to address that.

44
00:02:49,420 --> 00:02:53,020
Marie: And somehow motivate consumers to change, yes.

45
00:02:53,460 --> 00:02:58,720
Collin: One thing everyone's aware of is the need for a reduction in unnecessary packaging -

46
00:02:59,140 --> 00:03:04,600
but just about everything you buy in supermarkets is still covered in plastic.

47
00:03:05,140 --> 00:03:07,900
The government needs to do something about it.

48
00:03:08,030 --> 00:03:11,620
Marie: Absolutely. It's got to change.

49
00:03:11,740 --> 00:03:17,000
Collin: Do you think there'll be more interest in gluten- and lactose-free food?

50
00:03:17,380 --> 00:03:20,340
Marie: For people with allergies or food intolerances?

51
00:03:21,160 --> 00:03:27,460
I don't know. Lots of people I know have been buying that type of food for years now.

52
00:03:27,580 --> 00:03:30,940
Collin: Yes, even if they haven't been diagnosed with an allergy.

53
00:03:31,660 --> 00:03:39,260
Marie: That's right. One thing I've noticed is the number of branded products related to celebrity chefs -

54
00:03:39,940 --> 00:03:41,980
people watch them cooking on TV

55
00:03:41,980 --> 00:03:47,960
and then buy things like spice mixes or frozen foods with the chef's name on...

56
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:52,040
Marie: I bought something like that once, but I won't again.

57
00:03:52,700 --> 00:03:56,060
Collin: Yeah - I bought a ready-made spice mix for chicken

58
00:03:56,320 --> 00:04:00,960
which was supposed to be used by a chef I'd seen on television,

59
00:04:01,240 --> 00:04:02,980
and it didn't actually taste of anything.

60
00:04:04,640 --> 00:04:09,700
Marie: Mm. Did the article mention 'ghost kitchens' used to produce takeaway food?

61
00:04:09,880 --> 00:04:11,320
Collin: No. What are they?

62
00:04:11,780 --> 00:04:14,280
Marie: Well, they might have the name of a restaurant,

63
00:04:14,380 --> 00:04:19,020
but actually they're a cooking facility just for delivery meals

64
00:04:19,460 --> 00:04:21,620
the public don't ever go there.

65
00:04:21,830 --> 00:04:26,030
Marie: But people aren't aware of that - it's all kept very quiet.

66
00:04:26,030 --> 00:04:30,760
Collin: So people don't realise the food's not actually from the restaurant?

67
00:04:30,760 --> 00:04:31,400
Marie: Right.

68
00:04:32,980 --> 00:04:38,300
Collin: Did you know more and more people are using all sorts of different mushrooms now,

69
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:42,720
to treat different health concerns? Things like heart problems?

70
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:46,680
Marie: Hmm. They might be taking a big risk there.

71
00:04:47,280 --> 00:04:53,260
Collin: Yes, it's hard to know which varieties are safe to eat. Anyway maybe now...
